@h1 Your first document h1@

Create a file called @code hello.wit code@ and just write. No markup required —
a blank line starts a new paragraph.

@@@pre
Hello. This is a Wit document. It is only text,
and it reads like text.

A blank line starts a new paragraph. That is all
you need to begin.
pre@@@

@h2 Build it h2@

Turn it into a web page:

@@@pre
wit build hello.wit -o hello.html
pre@@@

…or a print-ready PDF:

@@@pre
wit build hello.wit -o hello.pdf
pre@@@

Open the result — it already looks like a real document, with no styling on
your part.

@h2 Add a little structure h2@

Reach for a heading and some emphasis when you want them; the rest stays prose.

@@@pre
@h1 Hello h1@

This is a Wit document. Add a heading when you want one,
and *bold* or _italics_ inline.
pre@@@

@h2 The whole loop h2@

Edit, run @code wit build code@, look at the output. That's the cycle. Keep the
@code .wit code@ file in @strong git strong@ and every draft you send a reader
is diff-able and versioned.
